==================================================================
DAY 15 - PART A: THEORY
The Kubernetes Network Model
Time: 30-40 minutes
==================================================================

1. THE THREE RULES
------------------
Kubernetes requires every network plugin to provide:
  1) Every pod gets its own IP address.
  2) Every pod can reach every other pod, on any node, WITHOUT NAT.
  3) Agents on a node (kubelet) can reach all pods on that node.

So a pod looks like a small VM on a flat network. No port mapping
between pods (unlike Docker's -p on Day 4).


2. HOW A POD GETS AN IP (on one node)
-------------------------------------
When the kubelet creates a pod:
  - the "pause" container creates a network namespace
  - the CNI plugin creates a veth pair: one end (eth0) inside the pod,
    the other end on the node
  - the plugin assigns an IP from the node's pod range (PodCIDR)

    +-------- node --------+
    |  pod A   pod B       |
    |  eth0    eth0        |
    |   |       |          |
    |  vethX   vethY       |
    |    \     /           |
    |   routes / bridge    |
    |       eth0 (node)    |
    +----------------------+

Each node owns a slice of the cluster pod network:
  cluster pod CIDR  10.244.0.0/16
  node 1            10.244.0.0/24
  node 2            10.244.1.0/24
  node 3            10.244.2.0/24


3. POD TO POD ACROSS NODES
--------------------------
The CNI plugin makes "10.244.2.0/24 lives on node 3" known to every
node. Ways to do it:
  - Plain routes (kindnet, Calico BGP mode)
  - Overlay tunnels: packets wrapped in VXLAN/Geneve (Flannel, Calico
    VXLAN, Cilium)
  - Cloud-native routing: pods get real VPC IPs (AWS VPC CNI, Azure
    CNI) - Week 7


4. WHY POD IPS ARE UNRELIABLE
-----------------------------
Pods are disposable. When a Deployment replaces a pod, the new pod
gets a NEW IP. Scaling adds and removes IPs constantly.

So you must never hard-code a pod IP. You need a stable address in
front of a changing group of pods: a SERVICE (Day 16).


5. THREE IP RANGES TO KNOW
--------------------------
  Node network     IPs of the machines   (kind: 172.18.0.x)
  Pod network      IPs of pods           (kind: 10.244.0.0/16)
  Service network  virtual IPs           (kind: 10.96.0.0/12)

They must not overlap with each other or with your company network.


6. CONTAINERS IN THE SAME POD
-----------------------------
Share one IP; they talk on localhost and must not use the same port
(you saw this on Day 9).


KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
----------------------
- Each pod has its own IP; pods reach each other without NAT.
- The CNI plugin wires it up (veth + routes or overlay).
- Pod IPs change; use Services for stable addresses.
